WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.556
The workbook's first chapter
turns the register into code,

2
00:00:03.556 --> 00:00:07.063
and its lab runs on fictional
audit records with no tenant.

3
00:00:07.063 --> 00:00:10.433
The check reads the register
itself: one writer per row,

4
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:14.403
every required transition present,
no deletes by provisioning,

5
00:00:14.403 --> 00:00:17.474
and the register signed
with its scope versioned.

6
00:00:17.474 --> 00:00:20.075
The program's own register
comes back clean.

7
00:00:20.075 --> 00:00:23.713
Then the scan compares a month of
audit records with the register.

8
00:00:23.713 --> 00:00:25.887
It finds eleven protected writes.

9
00:00:25.887 --> 00:00:28.940
Five came from the right
writer, one from the backstop,

10
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.825
four from a second writer, and one
from a writer the register forbids.

11
00:00:32.825 --> 00:00:35.409
So a help desk account disabled a leaver,

12
00:00:35.409 --> 00:00:39.950
a fix-up script wrote a coded slot,
and provisioning deleted an account.

13
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.200
Two limits apply.

14
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.700
The scan doesn't read Active Directory.

15
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.819
And the audit property names it
matches aren't documented by Microsoft,

16
00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:51.170
so confirm them in a test tenant
before you trust the scan.
